<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Velanati Chodas</h1><p class="st-meta">1076 CE – 1216 CE (approx.) · capital Tenali</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Telugu Choda family of the Krishna delta that rose as Chola vassals and briefly dominated coastal Andhra in the 12th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Velanati Chodas were a family of chiefs from Velanadu, the region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guntur">Guntur</a> in coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-region">Andhra</a>. In the late 11th century they rose as feudatories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulottunga-i">Kulottunga I</a>, who had absorbed the lands of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eastern-chalukyas">Eastern Chalukyas</a>, and governed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a> region on his behalf from Chandavolu, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenali">Tenali</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-region">Andhra</a> weakened in the 12th century, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonka-ii">Gonka II</a> and his successors ruled much of the coastal region between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-river">Krishna</a> and the Godavari independently, though still using Chola titles. They patronised Shaiva and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnava</a> temples and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telugu-language">Telugu</a> inscriptions record their grants. The kingdom was drawn into the wars of local chiefs, including the conflict remembered in the Telugu epic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-palnadu">battle of Palnadu</a> around 1182.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Weakened by these wars, the Velanati Chodas were defeated in the early 13th century by the Kakatiya king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganapati-kakatiya">Ganapati</a>, who absorbed coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-region">Andhra</a> into his kingdom around 1216.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1076 CE – 1216 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.11%;width:2.79%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1076 CE – 1216 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tenali</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gonka II, Kulottunga Rajendra Choda</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Telugu, Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chodas of Velanadu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1146&amp;focus=velanati-chodas" aria-label="Velanati Chodas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6877 1574 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Velanati Chodas lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7197 1830L7187 2016M7197 1830L7159 1767"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7187 2016h0M7159 1767h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7197 1830h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7197 1830h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tenali. <a href="/atlas?year=1146&amp;focus=velanati-chodas">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

